<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 3.6: Modeling Temperature and Population</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

<page>
  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-s">
    <title>Recall Properties and Newton's Law of Cooling</title>
    <sidebyside>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
             Relation: <m>{e^a = b \iff \ln(b) = a}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Inverses:
              <m>{\ln(e^x) = x, \ \ \ e^{\ln(x)}=x}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Multi. inside: <m>{\ln(a\cdot b) = \ln(a)+\ln(b)}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Division inside: <m>{\ln(\frac{a}{b})=\ln(a)-\ln(b)}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Powers come out: <m>{\ln(a^t)=t\ln(a)}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Long term: <m>{e^{kt} \rightarrow \infty, \ \ e^{-kt}\rightarrow 0}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        Temp of a warming/cooling object (<m>k\gt 0</m>):
        <md>
          F(t) = ae^{-kt}+c
        </md>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>c</m> is surrounding temp/long-term behavior
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>a</m> is <em>NOT</em> initial temp! But <m>F(0)=a+c</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k</m> is found from one other temp at <m>t\ne 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </sidebyside>
  </assemblage>
  

  <example workspace="3in">
    
    <statement>
      <p>You're cooking a turkey.  It is initially room temperature which is 
      <m>68^\circ</m> F.  You cook it in a <m>350^{\circ}</m> F oven.  
      After 50 minutes the turkey is <m>100^\circ</m> F.  What 
      is the temperature after 80 minutes?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-6-soln">
      <p>
        We know the following: our model has the form <m>F(t)=ae^{-kt}+c</m> (Newton's Law of Cooling).
        </p>
        <p>  
          The initial temperature is <m>68^\circ</m> so <m>F(0)=68</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The long-term temperature is the temp of the oven which is <m>350^\circ</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              When <m>t=50</m>, <m>F(50) = 100^\circ</m>.
              </p><p>
              Step 1: Long term temperature is long term behavior which is <m>c</m>, so <m>c=350</m>.
              The model then becomes <m>{F(t)=ae^{-kt}+350}</m>.
              </p><p>
          Step 2: Initial temp is not <m>a</m>, but <m>F(0)=68</m> and plugging 
          <m>0</m> into the model it is also <m>F(0)=a+350</m>, so 
          <m>68 = a+350</m>.  Rearranging, we get
        <m>a=68-350=-282</m>. Thus the model becomes
        <m>{F(t)=-282e^{-kt}+350}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Step 3: The last point.  We know <m>F(50)=100</m>, so plugging in
        <md>
          <mrow>100 \amp = -282e^{-50k}+350</mrow>
          <mrow>-250 \amp = -282e^{-50k}</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{250}{282} \amp = e^{-50k}</mrow>
          <mrow>\ln(\frac{250}{282}) \amp = {-50k}</mrow>
          <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{\ln(250/282)}{-50} </mrow>
  
        </md>
        So the entire model becomes 
        <md>
          F(t) = -282e^{-\frac{\ln(250/282)}{-50} t} + 350.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Now the final piece is that we want the temp after 80 minutes, 
        we want <m>F(80)</m>, so we plug in:
        <md>
          F(80) = -282e^{-\frac{\ln(250/282)}{-50} (80)} + 350.
        </md>
        In more sensible, approximate terms, <m>F(80)\approx 117.4</m>, so 
        the turkey is about <m>117.4^\circ</m> after 80 minutes.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  </page>

  <page>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.6.2</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>A can of soda is initially at room temperature, <m>72.3^{\circ}</m>
 Fahrenheit, and at time <m>t=0</m>
 is placed in a refrigerator set at <m>37.7^{\circ}</m>.
 In addition, we know that after <m>30</m>
 minutes, the soda’s temperature has dropped to <m>59.5^{\circ}</m>.
 Let <m>F(t)</m>
 represent the temperature of the soda in degrees Fahrenheit at time <m>t</m>
 in minutes.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use algebraic reasoning and your understanding of the physical situation to determine the exact values of <m>a</m>,
 <m>c</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 in the model <m>F(t)=ae^{-kt}+c</m>.</p>
        <p>(Find <m>c</m> then <m>a</m> then <m>k</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          Step 1: 
          The long-term temperature is the temperature of the refrigerator which is also <m>c</m> (the 
          horizontal asymptote/vertical shift), so
          <m>c=37.7</m>.  
          </p><p>Step 2: Initial value. Since <m>F(0)=a+37.7=72.3</m>, we get
          <m>a=72.3-37.7=34.6</m>.  So <m>F(t)=34.6e^{-kt}+37.7</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Step 3: Another point.
          We know <m>F(30)=59.5</m>, so we plug in and are left with 
          <m>k</m> as the only variable, which we can solve for:
          <md>
            <mrow>59.5 \amp = 34.6e^{-30k}+37.7</mrow>
            <mrow>21.8 \amp = 34.6e^{-30k}</mrow>
            <mrow>\dfrac{21.8}{34.6} \amp = e^{-30k}</mrow>
            <mrow>\ln(\dfrac{21.8}{34.6}) \amp = {-30k}</mrow>
            <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{\ln(21.8/34.6)}{-30}</mrow>
          </md>
          So we can plug that into our model, and we know an equation for <m>F(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Determine the exact time the object’s temperature is <m>42.4^{\circ}</m>.
 Clearly show your work. 
 Hint: it may help to leave <m>k</m> as <m>k</m> until you get <m>t=...</m>, then plug in <m>k</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          We're going to leave <m>k</m> as <m>k</m> for now, and then plug 
          it in at the very last step, because that seems a lot easier.  So 
          we want to find the time <m>t</m> when <m>F(t)=42.4</m> and note that 
          we are solving for <m>t</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>42.4 \amp = 34.6e^{-kt}+37.7 </mrow>
            <mrow>4.7 \amp = 34.6e^{-kt} </mrow>
            <mrow>\dfrac{4.7}{34.6} \amp =e^{-kt}</mrow>
            <mrow>\ln(\dfrac{4.7}{34.6}) \amp ={-kt}</mrow>
            <mrow>-kt \amp = \ln\left(\dfrac{4.7}{34.6}\right)</mrow>
            <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(4.7/34.6)}{-k}</mrow>
          </md>
          That seems nicer.  Now we can plug in <m>k=\dfrac{\ln(21.8/34.6)}{-30}</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>t = \dfrac{\ln(4.7/34.6)}{-(\dfrac{\ln(21.8/34.6)}{-30})}  </mrow>
          </md>
          Still gross, but kind of great at the same time.  It turns out this is approximately
          <m>t\approx 129.6</m> minutes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>In Desmos, enter the values you found for <m>a</m>,
 <m>c</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 in order to define the function <m>{F(t)=ae^{-kt}+c}</m>.
 Then, use Desmos to find the average rate of change of <m>F</m>
 on the interval <m>[25,30]</m>.
 What is the meaning (with units) of this value?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          The average rate of change on <m>[25,30]</m> is
          <md>
            AV_{[25,30]} = \dfrac{F(30)-F(25)}{30-25} \approx \dfrac{59.5-61.24}{5} \approx -0.349.
          </md>
          The units are <m>^\circ F/min</m>.  Between <m>t=25</m> and
          <m>t=30</m> minutes, the soda's temperature drops on
          average about <m>0.349^\circ</m> F each minute.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  </page>
<page>
<note>
<p><term>Reality check:</term> Populations don't usually grow forever with no limits, resources limit growth.  Calculus can help us develop a more realistic model called the <term>Logistic Model</term>.</p>
  
</note>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Logistic Model</title>
    <p>of population growth with positive constants <m>A, M, k</m>:
<md>P(t) = \frac{A}{1+Me^{-kt}}</md></p>
    <p>Since, <m>e^{-kt}\rightarrow 0</m>, then <m>\frac{A}{1+Me^{-kt}}\rightarrow \frac{A}{1+0} \rightarrow A</m>! Thus <m>P(t)\rightarrow A</m> in the long-term.</p>
    <p>** <m>A</m> is the <term>carrying capacity</term> of the population, i.e. the long-term behavior/max capacity.</p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.6.3. Logistic Model</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>In Desmos, define <m>P(t)=\frac{A}{1+Me^{-kt}}</m>
 and accept sliders for <m>A</m>,
 <m>M</m>,
 and <m>k</m>.
 Set the slider ranges for these parameters as follows: <m>0.01 \le A \le 10</m>;
 <m>0.01 \le M \le 10</m>;
 <m>0.01\le k \le 5</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch a typical graph of <m>P(t)</m>
 on the axes provided and write several sentences to explain the effects of <m>A</m>,
 <m>M</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 on the graph of <m>P</m>.</p>
       <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
      <shortdescription>
        A blank graph with horizontal axis for time t and vertical axis for population P.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-6-3">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">

          <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,12,12)">
            <grid-axes decorations="no" xlabel="t" ylabel="P" spacings="((-2,1,12),(-2,1,12))"/>


          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          See Desmos to see what it looks like, but it should look like and S-like curve.
          It is always increasing, and appears to level off over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>
              <m>A</m>: the carrying capacity, this acts as a vertical stretch and 
              a maximum output, so this also is the long-term behavior.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>M</m>: appears to affect the starting value, and acts similar to 
              a horizontal shift (left/right).  Not exactly, but it does affect the 
              horizontal direction only.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>k</m>: this appears to act like a horizontal stretch, and makes
              the S-curve either steeper or shallower depending on the value.
            </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>On a typical logistic graph, where does it appear that the population is growing most rapidly? How is this value connected to the carrying capacity, <m>A</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          
          The population appears grows most fastest (the steepest part of the
          graph), in the middle, which looks to be halfway between 0 and 
          the carrying capacity <m>A</m>, so around <m>y=A/2</m>.  
          Notice also this middle point appears to be where the graph changes from concave up to
          concave down.  
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your Desmos worksheet to find a logistic function <m>P</m>
 that has the following properties: <m>P(0)=2</m>,
 <m>P(2)=4</m>,
 and <m>P(t)</m>
 approaches <m>9</m> 
 as <m>t</m>
 increases without bound. What are the approximate values of <m>A</m>,
 <m>M</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 that make the function <m>P</m>
 fit these criteria?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          Somewhere around 
          <m>P(t)= \frac{9}{1+\frac72 e^{-0.516t}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

</page>
<page>
  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.6.4</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that a population of animals (measured in thousands) that lives on an island is known to grow according to the logistic model, where <m>t</m>
 is measured in years. We know the following information: <m>P(0) = 2.45</m>,
 <m>P(3)=4.52</m>,
 and given the resources availabe the island cannot support more than <m>11.7</m> thousand animals.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Determine the values of <m>A</m>,
 <m>M</m>,
 and <m>k</m> in the logist model below. Try to find exact. (For approximate, if you round, include 4 decimal places).
 <md>P(t) = \frac{A}{1+Me^{-kt}}.</md></p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          The order is actually very similar to solving Newton's Law of Cooling 
          Model.  Since <m>A</m> is the long-term behavior or eventual outcome, 
          this is the easiest to find and ideally the first step.  So <m>A = 11.7</m>, 
          and <m>{P(t)= \frac{11.7}{1+Me^{-kt}}}</m>.
          </p><p>
          Zeros are lovely, so just like in earlier problems we want to use 
          the initial condition (which is not equal to <m>M</m>!). 
          But <m>P(0)=2.45</m> so plugging in we have:
          <md>
            <mrow>2.45 \amp = \dfrac{11.7}{1+Me^0}</mrow>
            <mrow>2.45 \amp = \dfrac{11.7}{1+M}</mrow>
            </md>
            So getting the <m>M</m> out of the denominator by 
            multiplying both sides by <m>(1+M)</m>:
            <md>
            <mrow>2.45(1+M) \amp = 11.7</mrow>
            <mrow>2.45+2.45M \amp = 11.7</mrow>
            <mrow>2.45M \amp = 9.25</mrow>
            <mrow>M \amp = \dfrac{9.25}{2.45}</mrow>
          </md>
          so the model becomes <m>{P(t)= \frac{11.7}{1+\frac{9.25}{2.45}e^{-kt}}}</m>.
          </p><p>
          Now we only have one thing left to find, <m>k</m>, so we go to the 
          last piece of info which is the hardest (no zeros), and we know that 
          <m>P(3)=4.52</m>.  So plugging in (and sticking to the theme of solving exactly):
          <md>
            4.52 = \dfrac{11.7}{1+\frac{9.25}{2.45}e^{-3k}}
            </md>but to get the gross stuff out of the denominator we 
            can multiply both sides by the entire denom:
            <md>
            <mrow>4.52  ({1+\frac{9.25}{2.45}e^{-3k}}) \amp = 11.7 </mrow>
            <mrow>1+\frac{9.25}{2.45}e^{-3k} \amp = \frac{11.7}{4.52} </mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{9.25}{2.45}e^{-3k} \amp = \frac{11.7}{4.52} - 1 </mrow>
            <mrow>e^{-3k} \amp = \frac{2.45}{9.25}(\frac{11.7}{4.52} - 1) </mrow>
            <mrow>{-3k} \amp = \ln\left(\frac{2.45}{9.25}(\frac{11.7}{4.52} - 1)\right) </mrow>
            <mrow>{k} \amp = \frac{-1}{3}\ln\left(\frac{2.45}{9.25}(\frac{11.7}{4.52} - 1)\right) </mrow>
           
          </md>
          Eek.  This should be
          <m>k\approx 0.2886</m>.  Unless I have a typo, which is likely.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the approximate time value when the population will be <m>10</m>
 (thousand). Show your work.
 Hint: You may find it easier to not plug in <m>k</m> and <m>M</m> until you get <m>t=...</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          We will leave <m>k,M</m> and deal with them later!  That seems nicer.
          </p><p>
          So we need to solve for time <m>t</m> when <m>P(t)=10</m>, plugging in
          and remembering that we are trying to get <m>t=...</m>:
          
          <md>
            <mrow>10 \amp = \dfrac{11.7}{1+Me^{-kt}}</mrow>
            <mrow>10(1+Me^{-kt}) \amp = 11.7</mrow>
            <mrow>1+Me^{-kt} \amp = 1.17</mrow>
            <mrow>Me^{-kt} \amp = 0.17</mrow>
            <mrow>e^{-kt} \amp = \dfrac{0.17}{M}</mrow>
            <mrow>{-kt} \amp = \ln(\dfrac{0.17}{M})</mrow>
            <mrow>t \amp = -\frac{1}{k}\ln\left(\dfrac{0.17}{M}\right)</mrow>
          </md>
          Now we have <m>t</m>, let's plug in <m>M, k</m>, and let's just approximate here 
          since it doesn't ask for exact.  With the help of a calculator:
          <m>{t \approx 10.743}</m> years.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Plot your model from (a) and check that its values match the desired characteristics. Then, use Desmos to compute the average rate of change of <m>P</m>
 on the intervals <m>[0,2]</m>,
 <m>[2,4]</m>,
 <m>[4,6]</m>,
 and <m>[6,8]</m>.
 What is the meaning (with units) of the values you’ve found? How is the population growing on these intervals?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-6-soln">
        <p>
          See Desmos for a plot.
          We find
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>AV_{[0,2]}=\frac{P(2)-P(0)}{2-0}\approx0.65</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>AV_{[2,4]}\approx 0.796</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>AV_{[4,6]}\approx 0.836</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>AV_{[6,8]}\approx 0.747</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          All of these are in thousands with units of animals per year, so the first one says that 
          on average between years 0 and 2 the population was growing 
          by about 650 animals each year.
        </p>
        <p>
          The population always growing since the average rate of change 
          is always positive, but it seems to grow faster and then slow down at 
          some point.  
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>
</page>
</handout>
